+### Independent checks (fresh verifier /tmp/opencode/ref_zeroone.py, not the reporter's file)
+
+1. Scope: on informative k ~ n log n, N = n+k+1 = Theta(n log n), so n = Theta(N/log N) and
+   2^n = 2^{Theta(N/log N)} — exponential in unary length N. Recomputed n/(N/log2 N) ~ 1.2–1.25
+   for n=100..10000. The 0/1 principle shrinks a quantifier range (n! -> 2^n), not verification work.
+2. Separation-tree lemma: reconstructed from scratch. For any s distinct binary target orders a
+   comparison tree of height <= s-1 sorts them all by repeatedly comparing a pair on which two
+   remaining targets disagree. Enumerated all binary orders for n=4..7, took s=S(n)-1 targets,
+   30 random trials each (s=4,6,9,12 vs S(n)=5,7,10,13): height <= s-1 in every case, every
+   target sorted correctly, and a binary witness outside the target set was found that the tree
+   outputs wrongly.
+3. Unsoundness: for s <= k < S(n) (a no-instance) such a tree passes all s samples yet fails on a
+   witness, so a verifier accepting a height-k tree on fewer than S(n) sampled 0/1 inputs is
+   unsound. The reporter's LIMIT is correct: the argument does not extend to sample size >= S(n)
+   (star-set obstruction); larger samples are not claimed sound.
+4. Caveats handled honestly: Parberry coNP-hardness of sorting-program verification is correctly
+   flagged as NOT transferring to S(n) or a single optimal tree; non-optimality of known succinct
+   sorters is used as an open observation, not a theorem.
+
+### Remains open (not closed)
+
+Sample regime >= S(n); proof-based sub-route (poly-size poly-checkable correctness invariant for
+every optimal sorter, or a proof-size lower bound); SORT-BOUND-U in NP/coNP. No P-vs-NP consequence.
